3. Heuristic Definition & Admissibility Argument 
Heuristic Selection (Option A — Hop-Count Heuristic) 
3 
The chosen heuristic estimates the distance from any current node $n$ to the goal state 
$L4$ (Science Lab) by counting the minimum number of connection hops required to 
reach $L4$, ignoring edge weights: 
• $h(L4) = 0$ (Goal state) 
• $h(L2) = 1$ (1 hop: $L2 \rightarrow L4$) 
• $h(L5) = 1$ (1 hop: $L5 \rightarrow L4$) 
• $h(L1) = 2$ (2 hops: $L1 \rightarrow L2 \rightarrow L4$) 
• $h(L6) = 2$ (2 hops: $L6 \rightarrow L5 \rightarrow L4$) 
• $h(L3) = 2$ (2 hops: $L3 \rightarrow L2 \rightarrow L4$) 
Proof of Admissibility 
A heuristic function $h(n)$ is admissible if it never overestimates the true cost 
$h^*(n)$ required to reach the goal state from node $n$ (i.e., $h(n) \le h^*(n)$ for all $n$). 
Argument: 
Every direct edge connection in the campus graph has a physical length $c(u, v) \ge 
1\text{ km}$ (the smallest edge weight on the campus map is $1\text{ km}$ between 
$L1$ and $L2$). Because every single connection costs at least $1\text{ km}$, the true 
distance $h^*(n)$ to traverse $k$ hops is at least $k \times 1\text{ km} = k\text{ km}$. Since 
$h(n)$ equals the minimum hop count $k$, $h(n) \le h^*(n)$ holds universally across all 
states in this graph. Thus, the hop-count heuristic is strictly admissible and guarantees 
that A* Search returns an optimal path. 
4. Search Behavior & Comparative Analysis 
1. Breadth-First Search (BFS) 
• Frontier Strategy: Queue (First-In, First-Out / FIFO). 
• Behavior: BFS explores the search tree layer-by-layer according to edge depth 
(number of hops). It first checks all 1-hop neighbors from L1 (L2 and L6), then 
checks 2-hop neighbors. 
• Result: BFS found the path L1 -> L2 -> L4 (2 hops) with a cost of 3 km after 
expanding 4 nodes. On unweighted graphs, BFS is guaranteed to find the shortest 
path in terms of hop count. Because the minimum hop path on this graph also 
happens to be the shortest physical distance, BFS found the optimal path. 
2. Depth-First Search (DFS) 
• Frontier Strategy: Stack (Last-In, First-Out / LIFO). 
• Behavior: DFS follows a single path as deeply as possible before backtracking. 
From L1, when expanding neighbors in standard edge list order (L2 then L6), L2 is 
pushed to the stack and popped immediately. From L2, its unvisited neighbors (L3 
and L4) are pushed, and L4 is evaluated immediately. 
• Result: DFS reached L4 in 3 node expansions. Although DFS happened to find the 
optimal path L1 -> L2 -> L4 on this specific run, DFS does not guarantee 
optimality on weighted graphs and can easily return long, non-optimal paths 
depending on node ordering. 
3. A* Search 
• Frontier Strategy: Priority Queue (Min-Heap ordered by $f(n) = g(n) + h(n)$). 
• Behavior: A* combines accumulated travel distance $g(n)$ with estimated 
distance to goal $h(n)$. 
o At L1: $g=0, h=2 \implies f(L1) = 2$. 
o Neighbors expanded from L1: 
▪ L2: $g=1, h=1 \implies f(L2) = 2$ 
▪ L6: $g=3, h=2 \implies f(L6) = 5$ 
o L2 is popped next because $f(L2) = 2 < f(L6) = 5$. 
o From L2, L4 is generated: $g=1+2=3, h=0 \implies f(L4) = 3$. 
o L4 is popped and identified as the goal state. 
• Result: A* expanded only 3 nodes and immediately converged on the optimal 3 km 
path without ever exploring the suboptimal path through L6 (Cafeteria). 
5. Conclusion & Algorithm Preference 
On small, dense graphs like the CampusMind campus map, all three algorithms easily find 
the destination. However, A Search is superior* for campus navigation systems because: 
1. Unlike BFS, A* accounts for varying edge costs (kilometers) rather than just hop 
counts. 
2. Unlike DFS, A* mathematically guarantees the optimal path when used with an 
admissible heuristic. 
3. A* avoids evaluating unnecessary areas of campus by steering search expansion 
towards the target location. 
